[38;5;252m[1m## Two real defects, both in the verification setup[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The first run passed everything, but a negative control proved it was passing for the wrong reason.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mStale artifacts.[22m With [38;5;10mcrate-type = ["cdylib"][0m, integration tests don't depend on the lib target, so [38;5;10mcargo test[0m compiled only an rlib and never refreshed [38;5;10mlibbuffapp_lib.so[0m. Injected bugs went completely undetected because the tests kept dlopening an old artifact. Fixed three ways: added [38;5;10m"rlib"[0m to [38;5;10mcrate-type[0m, made the harness resolve the [38;5;10m.so[0m from its own test binary's profile and run [38;5;10mcargo build --lib[0m once per process, and added a mtime backstop that hard-fails if the [38;5;10m.so[0m predates [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mContaminated stdout capture.[22m Two buffering layers sit in front of fd 1 — libc's [38;5;10mstdout[0m (used by [38;5;10mprintf[0m in both [38;5;10m.so[0ms) and Rust's [38;5;10mLineWriter[0m (used by libtest for progress lines). An unflushed libtest fragment was landing inside the redirect window, producing a stray leading [38;5;10m\n[0m on the C side and a false mismatch. Fixed by draining both layers around the capture; [38;5;10mcompare[0m additionally requires a mismatch to reproduce three times, since a real divergence is deterministic and contamination is not.[0m[0m
